WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.766 --> 00:00:03.102
♪ ♪

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:04.670
PETER HAMMERSTEDT:
We have to do this

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.839
under the cover of darkness.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:08.975
There could be eyes watching us.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:11.310
It's only through
a stealth mission, really,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.445
that we'll have any success

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.414
intercepting
an illegal operator.

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:17.116
MARIANA VAN ZELLER:
I'm on the trail

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:20.452
of what some consider
the perfect crime.

00:00:20.452 --> 00:00:25.792
A black market worth
more than $20 billion,

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:29.362
with little risk
of getting caught.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:32.231
I'm told it's
like robbing a bank

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:36.135
in the middle of the ocean.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:38.004
So you see these lights
out there on the ocean,

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.439
those are the six
super trawlers.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.374
PETER: One of the reasons
that we're all black

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:44.043
is so that these super trawlers

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:45.578
don't see that a patrol
is under way,

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:48.348
that they don't see us
leaving the harbor.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.583
MARIANA: Somewhere out there
in the darkness

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:55.355
are the most prolific
wildlife poachers on the planet.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:57.923
Who are they?
How do they work?

00:00:57.923 --> 00:01:01.360
And why is it so hard
to stop them?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.996
I thought I knew black markets,

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.966
but I've never seen
anything like this.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:11.337
♪ ♪

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:15.341
♪ ♪

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:19.178
PETER: In this mass
expanse of water,

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.480
we're looking for ships

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.049
that are doing everything
that they can to hide.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.452
♪ ♪

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.254
MARIANA: I've come to Liberia

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.156
to understand how
illegal fishing works.

00:01:31.156 --> 00:01:33.192
PETER: These trawlers
come in direct conflict

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.260
with the local fishermen,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.328
because they're
literally stealing fish

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:38.631
from the local communities here.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.632
MARIANA: Some countries
have the resources

00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:43.536
to protect their own waters
from poachers;

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:44.971
others don't.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.373
That's why Liberia
has enlisted the help

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:51.311
of an international organization
called Sea Shepherd.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:55.548
On board are a 30-person crew,
the Liberian Coast Guard

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.117
and my guide
to this joint patrol,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:02.321
20-year Sea Shepherd veteran
Peter Hammerstedt.

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:03.723
Peter currently heads

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.825
Sea Shepherd's task force
in Africa,

00:02:06.825 --> 00:02:09.896
a region plagued by overfishing.

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:11.430
What's causing the overfishing?

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.132
PETER: You have a European fleet

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.335
that's at a massive
overcapacity,

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:18.871
two and a half times
what it could sustainably fish

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:20.239
in European waters.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:22.007
You have a Chinese
distant water fishing fleet

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:25.778
that has essentially wiped out
fisheries off the coast of China

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.580
and that is now seeking
waters to fish elsewhere.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.083
And these two
massive fishing fleets

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.151
from Europe, from China,

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.786
have congregated in areas

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.389
where, historically,
there still has been fish.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.491
Places like West Africa.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:42.662
MARIANA:
There's no global framework

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:46.299
that governs what is legal
in the fishing industry.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.801
That's a huge part
of the problem.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.603
And in places like West Africa,

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:54.274
foreign trawlers can take
advantage of patchy regulation,

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.641
poor enforcement

00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:59.512
and the rampant corruption
of some local officials.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:03.149
It's a low-risk,
high-reward crime,

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:07.287
but the Sea Shepherd
is trying to change that.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:09.222
PETER: Six miles offshore,
no industrial vessels

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.858
are allowed inside
of an inshore exclusion zone.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:14.693
But when there's no
patrolling presence at sea,

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:18.164
these trawlers routinely turn
off their location transponders,

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:19.932
and they go in.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:22.936
MARIANA: Every large commercial
fishing vessel is equipped

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:27.306
with an Automatic
Identification System, or AIS,

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:30.610
which transmits
its position at sea.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.378
If I were to be
fishing illegal

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.846
inside the exclusion zones,

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.282
as a captain of a ship,
I would turn off the AIS?

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.883
PETER: You'd get close
to the prohibited area.

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:39.919
That's when you would
turn your AIS off.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.587
And you wouldn't
reappear on AIS

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:45.191
until you've left
that prohibited area.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.126
MARIANA: In order
to directly observe

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.828
suspicious fishing vessels,

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:53.233
we need to get closer to them
and avoid being detected.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:57.536
That means we wait for the cover
of nightfall again.

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:01.473
♪ ♪

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:04.711
WOMAN: So these are the vessels
that we are looking at.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:07.680
At the moment they are about
three miles away from us.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.681
MARIANA: We're super close
to this trawler, right?

00:04:09.681 --> 00:04:12.085
WOMAN: At the moment, yes.
We can see them.

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:13.152
MARIANA: Oh, you can
actually see them?

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.353
PETER: Yeah.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:15.854
MARIANA: Oh, it's that,
what, light over there.

00:04:15.854 --> 00:04:16.889
WOMAN: Yes.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:18.190
MARIANA: So that's the boat
we're going after?

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.525
WOMAN: Yes.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:22.061
MARIANA: The Sea Shepherd crew
has found and identified

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:26.866
a trawler with a known history
of turning off its AIS.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.435
What have you guys been able
to find out about this boat?

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.270
PETER: There's not that much
known about this vessel,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.706
which is suspicious
in and of itself.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.509
We know that this vessel has
a long history of dark periods,

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.510
where they turn off
their location transponder.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.747
And we know that they've done
some suspicious identity changes

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:45.617
in, especially, claiming
to be a Somali vessel,

00:04:45.617 --> 00:04:48.221
as soon as
a month and a half ago.

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:49.489
Uh, this is a vessel

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.758
that we think is most likely
flagged to Senegal,

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:56.562
but with a beneficial owner
that's in Spain.

00:04:56.562 --> 00:05:01.567
MARIANA: Peter has a long
history with the Spanish.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.369
Back in the early 2000s,

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.271
one out of every three fish
in the Antarctic

00:05:06.271 --> 00:05:08.307
was being caught illegally...

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:11.143
most by Spanish-owned vessels.

00:05:11.143 --> 00:05:15.547
Some were brought to justice,
but six trawlers kept at it,

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:18.217
evading authorities
by changing their colors,

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.320
their flags
and even their names.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.290
They were known
as the Bandit 6.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.426
The most notorious, a vessel
that made millions

00:05:27.426 --> 00:05:31.496
poaching Chilean sea bass,
was known as the Thunder.

00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:33.833
And Peter helped bring it down.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.568
PETER: We found the Thunder
fishing in a CCAMLR region.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.036
They're not licensed
to fish down here.

00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:40.740
There's an Interpol Purple
Notice issued for this vessel.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.675
We've told the vessel
that they're under arrest

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:46.245
and we've told them
to go to Fremantle, Australia.

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:49.648
We saw this vessel
emerge out of the fog.

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:52.718
PETER: That would begin
what ultimately would become

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.721
the longest maritime pursuit
in history.

00:05:55.721 --> 00:05:57.623
The chase stretched
across three oceans,

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:02.361
covering 11,000 nautical miles,
and covered 110 days.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:05.464
(ship horn blowing)

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:06.866
MARIANA: And then
in the end what happened?

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.902
PETER: Ultimately, unable
to shake us as the pursuers,

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:13.438
the captain of the Thunder
decided to sink his own ship,

00:06:13.438 --> 00:06:16.241
in an attempt to destroy
the evidence on board.

00:06:16.241 --> 00:06:17.476
MARIANA: Were they able to?

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:19.078
PETER:
As they abandoned the ship,

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:21.747
three of my crew boarded
the vessel with cameras.

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:24.984
They were able to seize
nautical charts, mobile phones,

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:26.252
computer hard drives,

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:28.153
evidence that was
turned over to Interpol,

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:32.291
who then used that evidence
to chase down the owner.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:35.027
She's going down.

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:37.330
MARIANA: Although several
crew members of the Thunder

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.764
were sent to prison,

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:43.136
the shipowner, like the owners
of the other Bandit 6 vessels,

00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:45.604
remained free.

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:48.808
And Peter is confident that many
of those same players

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:52.311
are still active in
the illegal fishing game today.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:53.845
WOMAN: At the moment,
they cannot see us.

00:06:53.845 --> 00:06:56.748
They can probably see
a very small red light,

00:06:56.748 --> 00:06:59.318
but not able to ID what we are.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.919
PETER: And we've also
reduced the speed,

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:03.589
so we're matching their speed
of about three knots,

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.624
which is a speed
indicative of fishing.

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:07.293
So if they're just looking
at us on radar,

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:10.229
they're going to think
that we're another trawler.

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:11.597
MARIANA: Mmm.

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:14.400
So it's the perfect way to
approach a situation like this,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.202
for a covert mission
like this one, I guess?

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:18.403
PETER: The perfect way
to surprise a fishing boat

00:07:18.403 --> 00:07:20.273
is to pretend that you
are a fishing boat.

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.474
MARIANA: Hmm.

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:25.344
PETER: The plan tonight

00:07:25.344 --> 00:07:27.913
is that we'll be assisting
the Liberian Coast Guard.

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:30.917
Kanbal III claims to be
a shrimp trawler

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:33.619
but may be doing something
very, very different.

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:36.155
MARIANA: In 2017,

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:39.692
the Kanbal III was approached
by another joint patrol,

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:43.996
conducted by Senegalese fishing
authorities and Greenpeace.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:46.899
For more than 20 minutes,
Kanbal III's captain

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:49.668
ignored radio requests
to slow down

00:07:49.668 --> 00:07:51.103
and to allow inspection.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:53.005
(man shouting)

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:55.941
MARIANA: Once the Senegalese
authorities finally boarded,

00:07:55.941 --> 00:07:57.776
they discovered
Kanbal III's nets

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:01.447
had been manipulated to create
a smaller mesh size,

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:04.050
thereby capturing more fish.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.486
This is illegal
in most of the world.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:08.987
They also discovered
that the crew

00:08:08.987 --> 00:08:12.158
had attempted to undo
their net modifications

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:13.459
prior to inspection.

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:19.065
(man shouting)

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.201
MARIANA: How dangerous
are these operations?

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:34.446
WOMAN: What we are doing
carries some risks.

00:08:34.446 --> 00:08:37.883
But we have the Liberian
Coast Guard with us.

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:42.388
We have a trained crew, and
that's what we are here for.

00:08:42.388 --> 00:08:45.057
(radio chatter)

00:08:45.057 --> 00:08:48.894
MARIANA: Being in a small boat
on the open ocean at night

00:08:48.894 --> 00:08:51.464
is an unnerving experience.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:53.866
It's cold,
you can't see a thing,

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:58.337
and you're surrounded on
all sides by deep, dark water.

00:08:58.337 --> 00:09:01.474
♪ ♪

00:09:01.474 --> 00:09:03.275
It's scary.

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:05.944
You feel small and helpless.

00:09:05.944 --> 00:09:08.480
♪ ♪

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.783
As we get closer
to the Kanbal III,

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:14.120
I'm struck by the danger
of what we're about to do.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.290
(man speaking foreign language)

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.559
MARIANA: Not only are we
attempting to board a ship

00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:22.861
in the open ocean at night,

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:27.600
but we have no idea what
or who we'll find on board.

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:35.908
♪ ♪

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:41.581
♪ ♪

00:09:44.883 --> 00:09:51.089
♪ ♪

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:57.329
♪ ♪

00:09:57.329 --> 00:10:01.934
(officer shouting)

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:03.869
MARIANA: I'm with the crew
of the Sea Shepherd

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:05.704
and the Liberian Coast Guard

00:10:05.704 --> 00:10:08.640
as they board a fishing trawler
called the Kanbal III

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.543
that's been exhibiting
suspicious behavior.

00:10:11.543 --> 00:10:15.114
♪ ♪

00:10:21.587 --> 00:10:22.754
MARIANA: Pedro.

00:10:22.754 --> 00:10:25.057
So, the captain
is actually Portuguese.

00:10:25.057 --> 00:10:27.159
I just saw his passport
right here.

00:11:19.145 --> 00:11:20.046
MARIANA: Certa.

00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:37.963
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:11:42.134 --> 00:11:43.969
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:46.805
He seems uncomfortable
with my questions,

00:11:46.805 --> 00:11:48.874
which makes me even more curious

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:51.143
about what might be found
on the boat.

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:13.665
MARIANA: Okay.

00:12:13.665 --> 00:12:15.767
We go over the ship's documents,

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:17.069
and it looks like their license

00:12:17.069 --> 00:12:20.172
allows them to trawl
for shrimp and crab.

00:12:20.172 --> 00:12:22.707
But the Liberian Coast Guard
needs to see proof

00:12:22.707 --> 00:12:25.310
that they're fishing
by the rules.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:28.981
So they ask the captain
to reel in the nets.

00:12:30.983 --> 00:12:35.921
Oh, wow. So you can see the fish
starting to come in.

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:39.024
They're opening up the nets?

00:12:39.024 --> 00:12:40.058
Oh, wow.

00:12:40.058 --> 00:12:44.696
♪ ♪

00:12:44.696 --> 00:12:48.867
♪ ♪

00:12:48.867 --> 00:12:51.070
Okay, and this is essentially
what they have

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.339
the actual license
to be fishing right now,

00:12:53.339 --> 00:12:54.306
is the shrimp.

00:12:54.306 --> 00:12:57.510
I mean, I grew up
eating this, you know,

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:58.810
this is called lagostim
in Portugal.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.746
I grew up eating
this kind of shrimp.

00:13:00.746 --> 00:13:03.415
PETER: Here the shrimp
is maybe not even 1/10

00:13:03.415 --> 00:13:04.483
of what the catch is, right?

00:13:04.483 --> 00:13:07.519
And that's pretty typical
of a shrimp fishery,

00:13:07.519 --> 00:13:10.689
this enormous amount of bycatch.

00:13:10.689 --> 00:13:12.524
MARIANA:
Bycatch refers to the fish

00:13:12.524 --> 00:13:16.261
or other marine species
that are caught unintentionally

00:13:16.261 --> 00:13:19.064
while fishing
for a specific target.

00:13:19.064 --> 00:13:22.734
In this case, shrimp.

00:13:22.734 --> 00:13:24.336
To me, you know,
looking at some of these

00:13:24.336 --> 00:13:26.938
and knowing that each
of these shrimp in Portugal

00:13:26.938 --> 00:13:27.973
on a plate at a restaurant,

00:13:27.973 --> 00:13:30.408
they can sell
for about 20 euros each,

00:13:30.408 --> 00:13:33.045
and you can see the amount
of money that can be made,

00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:35.213
not by these guys,
but by the big bosses, right?

00:13:35.213 --> 00:13:38.417
The people that are actually
making the money from, you know,

00:13:38.417 --> 00:13:40.852
thousands and thousands
and thousands of dollars

00:13:40.852 --> 00:13:43.388
that we're seeing worth
of shrimp on this boat.

00:13:43.388 --> 00:13:47.025
PETER: And here these guys
are making $150 a month.

00:13:47.025 --> 00:13:51.596
This is not--like, the economics
of this does not trickle down.

00:13:51.596 --> 00:13:53.799
MARIANA: It's an ugly reality,

00:13:53.799 --> 00:13:57.036
but still no sign
of illegal activity.

00:13:57.036 --> 00:13:59.804
So, I make my way
down to the cargo hold,

00:13:59.804 --> 00:14:02.841
where the catch
is frozen and kept.

00:14:02.841 --> 00:14:06.545
So, it says cangrejos, crab;
gamba, shrimp;

00:14:06.545 --> 00:14:10.916
and, uh, captured in the
Atlantic, so very, very vague.

00:14:10.916 --> 00:14:13.084
And then heading
towards Vigo, España,

00:14:13.084 --> 00:14:14.586
and it says the importer...

00:14:14.586 --> 00:14:15.620
yeah, which is a Spanish name.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:16.855
Have you heard of this company?

00:14:16.855 --> 00:14:19.158
WOMAN: Yeah, yeah.
We've heard of them before.

00:14:19.158 --> 00:14:24.362
Based in Vigo, which is a city
in Galicia, a region of Spain.

00:14:24.362 --> 00:14:27.132
PETER: You could tell
the captain was a bit evasive.

00:14:27.132 --> 00:14:29.734
He didn't want to say that
the real ownership of the vessel

00:14:29.734 --> 00:14:30.936
is in Spain.

00:14:30.936 --> 00:14:33.472
There's Spanish language
documents on the bridge,

00:14:33.472 --> 00:14:36.141
there's documentation
on the cartons of fish

00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:38.777
that everything's
being exported to Spain.

00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:40.445
To Vigo, Spain,
which is Galicia,

00:14:40.445 --> 00:14:44.182
which is really the heart of
fishing in Spain, in particular.

00:14:44.182 --> 00:14:47.085
MARIANA: And yet,
despite Peter's suspicions,

00:14:47.085 --> 00:14:49.788
there's no evidence
of illegal activity.

00:14:49.788 --> 00:14:54.860
PETER: Today the Liberian Coast
Guard has to let this ship go.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.961
MARIANA: So the nets
of the Kanbal III

00:14:56.961 --> 00:14:59.531
will soon be back in the water.

00:14:59.531 --> 00:15:02.200
♪ ♪

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.103
I spent almost a week
on the Sea Shepherd

00:15:05.103 --> 00:15:08.606
and learned one of the great
ironies of the fishing world--

00:15:08.606 --> 00:15:12.677
that despite the fact that
almost 20% of our seafood

00:15:12.677 --> 00:15:14.446
comes from the black market,

00:15:14.446 --> 00:15:18.883
it's nearly impossible
to catch anyone red-handed.

00:15:18.883 --> 00:15:20.585
But I'm not giving up.

00:15:20.585 --> 00:15:22.387
I'm determined to find my way

00:15:22.387 --> 00:15:25.524
inside this world
of illegal fishing.

00:15:33.265 --> 00:15:36.168
PAUL WOODS: Yeah, this is
the global footprint of fishing,

00:15:36.168 --> 00:15:37.635
mostly industrial fishing.

00:15:37.635 --> 00:15:39.203
MARIANA: That's all the green
that I'm seeing right here,

00:15:39.203 --> 00:15:40.204
all these little green squares?

00:15:40.204 --> 00:15:41.273
PAUL: Yeah, each one of these

00:15:41.273 --> 00:15:42.541
is an area where vessels
are fishing.

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:43.475
MARIANA: Uh-huh.

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:45.977
PAUL: There's 50,000
vessels out there.

00:15:45.977 --> 00:15:47.078
MARIANA: 50,000?
PAUL: Doing, yeah.

00:15:47.078 --> 00:15:48.079
MARIANA: Industrial, commercial?

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:49.348
PAUL: Yeah, just the big ones.

00:15:49.348 --> 00:15:50.382
MARIANA: 50,000?

00:15:50.382 --> 00:15:52.684
PAUL: Yeah, right.
50,000 vessels around the world.

00:15:52.684 --> 00:15:56.821
MARIANA: Perhaps as much as 20%
of seafood brought into the U.S.

00:15:56.821 --> 00:15:58.857
comes from the black market.

00:15:58.857 --> 00:16:02.360
Yet, I'm still searching
for a way inside.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:05.931
I'm hoping Paul Woods can
point me in the right direction.

00:16:05.931 --> 00:16:08.400
He's the co-founder
of Global Fishing Watch,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.104
a non-profit that uses
state-of-the-art data analysis

00:16:12.104 --> 00:16:15.207
to monitor fishing vessels
around the world.

00:16:15.207 --> 00:16:18.710
So if you are doing
illegal activities out at sea,

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:21.246
what are the chances you'll
actually be caught, in general?

00:16:21.246 --> 00:16:23.248
PAUL: In well-regulated
countries,

00:16:23.248 --> 00:16:24.582
there's a good chance
you'll get caught.

00:16:24.582 --> 00:16:27.952
This is clear because
the U.S. is good at managing.

00:16:27.952 --> 00:16:29.821
In Hawaii there's lots
of fish out here,

00:16:29.821 --> 00:16:32.824
but they, uh, the, you know,
the U.S. Navy keeps them out.

00:16:32.824 --> 00:16:35.094
Whereas, uh, in Africa,
in West Africa,

00:16:35.094 --> 00:16:38.764
all the foreign fleets,
Spain and China, Korea, Japan,

00:16:38.764 --> 00:16:40.799
and the United States,
you know,

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:42.000
they all just fish in there,

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:43.669
and they're not,
they don't keep them out,

00:16:43.669 --> 00:16:45.170
because they're not good
at keeping them out.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:48.439
We've had issues where
we've seen vessels on the map

00:16:48.439 --> 00:16:50.275
doing something
we know is illegal,

00:16:50.275 --> 00:16:53.244
we report it to the authorities,
and we tell them,

00:16:53.244 --> 00:16:55.213
"This vessel is fishing
in your waters,

00:16:55.213 --> 00:16:58.150
it's not on your registry list,
it's clearly illegal,"

00:16:58.150 --> 00:16:59.518
and then we find out

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:02.354
that they were retroactively
granted a license to fish.

00:17:02.354 --> 00:17:04.156
So this happens routinely.

00:17:04.156 --> 00:17:05.723
MARIANA: Hmm. And why
do you think that is?

00:17:05.723 --> 00:17:08.961
PAUL: Uh, well, because there's,
there's money to be made

00:17:08.961 --> 00:17:12.463
in, uh, fishing
and, uh, licensing fishing.

00:17:12.463 --> 00:17:15.266
You know, and that is,
you know, more powerful

00:17:15.266 --> 00:17:19.171
than the need to keep
the fishery sustainable.

00:17:19.171 --> 00:17:21.205
MARIANA: How frustrating
is it for you?

00:17:21.205 --> 00:17:23.575
PAUL: Uh, it's,
it's pretty frustrating.

00:17:23.575 --> 00:17:28.713
What you need is
for the fishers to know

00:17:28.713 --> 00:17:31.383
that eventually, even if they're
not caught in the act,

00:17:31.383 --> 00:17:34.719
they're going to, you know,
face consequences

00:17:34.719 --> 00:17:37.055
for breaking the rules.

00:17:37.055 --> 00:17:40.024
MARIANA: But right now,
that's not happening.

00:17:40.024 --> 00:17:41.726
In fact, the criminals are using

00:17:41.726 --> 00:17:45.997
the globalized nature of
the industry to their advantage.

00:17:45.997 --> 00:17:47.432
PAUL: The vessel fishes
in one country,

00:17:47.432 --> 00:17:49.300
it lands the catch
in another country,

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.569
it's flagged in a third country,

00:17:50.569 --> 00:17:52.170
and it's owned by someone
in a fourth country,

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.805
and that's where
all the money goes.

00:17:53.805 --> 00:17:55.807
So how can you even police it?

00:17:55.807 --> 00:17:59.544
There's four countries involved
that all have to cooperate

00:17:59.544 --> 00:18:03.815
in order to actually create
consequences for that owner

00:18:03.815 --> 00:18:06.551
for their illegal actions
that happen on the water.

00:18:06.551 --> 00:18:09.120
MARIANA: That's why
some shipowners take advantage

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.723
of what's called
"flags of convenience."

00:18:11.723 --> 00:18:14.125
They register their vessel
in a foreign country

00:18:14.125 --> 00:18:15.961
with limited oversight,

00:18:15.961 --> 00:18:18.997
often as a way to skirt
regulations at home

00:18:18.997 --> 00:18:21.099
or avoid detection abroad.

00:18:21.099 --> 00:18:24.169
PAUL:
It's a global supply chain.

00:18:24.169 --> 00:18:25.671
Everything gets mingled
and flows around.

00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:29.107
So once, once it comes off
the boat and goes into that,

00:18:29.107 --> 00:18:31.042
it's really hard to trace.

00:18:31.042 --> 00:18:37.082
♪ ♪

00:18:37.082 --> 00:18:39.651
♪ ♪

00:18:39.651 --> 00:18:41.820
MARIANA: The shrimp I saw
on the Kanbal III

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.989
is bound for Vigo, Spain,

00:18:43.989 --> 00:18:47.192
home to the largest
fishing port in Europe.

00:18:49.494 --> 00:18:53.632
Every year, more than
700,000 tons of catch

00:18:53.632 --> 00:18:55.300
are offloaded here.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:58.370
The fish are sorted,
sold and distributed

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:02.173
to countries around the globe,
including the U.S.

00:19:02.173 --> 00:19:04.209
It's a massive operation.

00:19:04.209 --> 00:19:10.682
High-tech, spotlessly clean,
rigorously controlled.

00:19:10.682 --> 00:19:14.686
Every crate of fish is weighed,
catalogued, and barcoded,

00:19:14.686 --> 00:19:18.656
so buyers know exactly
when and where it was caught.

00:19:18.656 --> 00:19:21.460
(speaking Spanish)

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.930
MARIANA:
That's the theory, anyway.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:29.367
In reality, any one of the
hundreds of species on sale here

00:19:29.367 --> 00:19:31.102
could have been
transferred at sea

00:19:31.102 --> 00:19:33.204
from an illegal fishing vessel

00:19:33.204 --> 00:19:35.106
to a vessel carrying
the paperwork

00:19:35.106 --> 00:19:37.842
to make its cargo
look legitimate.

00:19:37.842 --> 00:19:40.746
(speaking Spanish)

00:19:40.746 --> 00:19:42.814
MARIANA: Once the contraband
fish has been mixed

00:19:42.814 --> 00:19:45.550
with a legal catch
in a ship's freezers,

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:48.653
it's impossible
to tell them apart.

00:19:48.653 --> 00:19:51.656
It's one common way
that illegal seafood

00:19:51.656 --> 00:19:55.793
gets laundered
into the legal supply chain.

00:19:55.793 --> 00:19:58.529
But I haven't come
to Spain for the fish.

00:19:58.529 --> 00:20:01.867
I've come for the fishermen.

00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:07.806
The ones that have given Galicia
its rather notorious reputation.

00:20:07.806 --> 00:20:11.877
PETER: Galicia has always been
a smuggling portal for Europe.

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:14.746
They've been involved in
trafficking not just of fish,

00:20:14.746 --> 00:20:17.048
but of drugs and tobacco

00:20:17.048 --> 00:20:19.484
and other illicit
products as well.

00:20:19.484 --> 00:20:22.053
The companies behind vessels
involved in the poaching

00:20:22.053 --> 00:20:24.489
of Patagonian
and Antarctic toothfish,

00:20:24.489 --> 00:20:27.859
they're known
as the Galician Mafia.

00:20:27.859 --> 00:20:29.094
MARIANA: I want to find someone

00:20:29.094 --> 00:20:31.629
who's worked
on these illegal vessels

00:20:31.629 --> 00:20:36.801
and knows the secrets
of this dirty trade firsthand.

00:20:36.801 --> 00:20:40.471
After asking around a bit,
I meet a retired fisherman

00:20:40.471 --> 00:20:43.709
who says he'll
give it to me straight.

00:20:57.222 --> 00:21:00.492
♪ ♪

00:21:17.843 --> 00:21:20.445
MARIANA: Perico is
a retired fisherman.

00:21:20.445 --> 00:21:24.382
He says he spent 35 years
working on Spanish vessels

00:21:24.382 --> 00:21:26.618
in the frigid North Atlantic.

00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:28.920
Perico tells me
that his career at sea

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:32.324
was marked by constant
illegal activity.

00:21:44.202 --> 00:21:45.470
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:22:31.148 --> 00:22:32.517
MARIANA: But when I press Perico

00:22:32.517 --> 00:22:36.621
for the names of the worst
offenders, he clams up.

00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:45.397
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:22:47.565 --> 00:22:49.401
MARIANA: And there's the rub.

00:22:49.401 --> 00:22:53.771
Spanish fishing is,
at its heart, a family business.

00:22:53.771 --> 00:23:00.612
A very big family business
that keeps its secrets close.

00:23:00.612 --> 00:23:03.514
But then, a new lead.

00:23:03.514 --> 00:23:05.784
Remember the Bandit 6?

00:23:05.784 --> 00:23:08.853
One of which the Sea Shepherd
chased for 110 days

00:23:08.853 --> 00:23:12.089
before the captain
purposely sank his boat?

00:23:12.089 --> 00:23:15.794
Well, I was able to connect
with a local journalist

00:23:15.794 --> 00:23:18.896
who has access to
a treasure trove of documents

00:23:18.896 --> 00:23:21.666
relating to the investigation
of those vessels

00:23:21.666 --> 00:23:24.269
and the ensuing court case.

00:23:24.269 --> 00:23:25.803
If I want to find a source

00:23:25.803 --> 00:23:28.206
deeper in this
illegal fishing world,

00:23:28.206 --> 00:23:30.776
this could be my chance.

00:23:30.776 --> 00:23:34.145
How does the fact that
we're talking about these,

00:23:34.145 --> 00:23:35.614
you know, very wealthy companies

00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:38.649
with fleets of boats
and fancy websites

00:23:38.649 --> 00:23:41.653
and a lot of lawyers, how does
that complicate matters for you

00:23:41.653 --> 00:23:44.789
in terms of being a journalist
and investigating this?

00:23:44.789 --> 00:23:47.859
MAN: I have to be careful,
and, uh...

00:23:50.862 --> 00:23:51.896
MARIANA: Can you tell me
a little bit

00:23:51.896 --> 00:23:53.798
about what all these
documents are?

00:23:53.798 --> 00:23:56.401
MAN: Those documents
are the outcome

00:23:56.401 --> 00:23:59.404
of the, probably
the best investigation

00:23:59.404 --> 00:24:00.905
on illegal fishing.

00:24:00.905 --> 00:24:02.407
8,000 pages.

00:24:02.407 --> 00:24:03.574
MARIANA: 8,000 pages?

00:24:03.574 --> 00:24:05.010
MAN: 8,000 pages.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:08.046
The investigation
was led by Interpol,

00:24:08.046 --> 00:24:11.550
together with
the Spanish Guardia Civil,

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:12.751
on the Bandit 6.

00:24:12.751 --> 00:24:14.018
MARIANA: I mean, they were
caught red-handed.

00:24:14.018 --> 00:24:15.687
There's plenty of evidence
that they were,

00:24:15.687 --> 00:24:17.755
what they were doing
was illegal, right?

00:24:17.755 --> 00:24:21.526
MAN: Everything is proved here,
in all the documents,

00:24:21.526 --> 00:24:25.596
that those Bandit 6
were committing crimes.

00:24:25.596 --> 00:24:28.199
MARIANA: Incredibly,
the Spanish Supreme Court

00:24:28.199 --> 00:24:32.436
overturned the case against
the shipowners of the Bandit 6.

00:24:32.436 --> 00:24:33.704
They decided that,

00:24:33.704 --> 00:24:36.474
while the Bandit 6
did commit illegal activity,

00:24:36.474 --> 00:24:38.776
the courts did not have
jurisdiction,

00:24:38.776 --> 00:24:41.279
because those
illegal acts occurred

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:44.382
in international waters.

00:24:44.382 --> 00:24:46.584
MAN: Here, we have
all the coordinators

00:24:46.584 --> 00:24:49.554
of the fishing activities
from Galicia.

00:24:49.554 --> 00:24:51.422
MARIANA: Wow. So, these
are the names and contacts?

00:24:51.422 --> 00:24:52.657
MAN: Yeah.
MARIANA: All the coordinators?

00:24:52.657 --> 00:24:53.791
MAN: That's it. That's it.

00:24:53.791 --> 00:24:55.292
MARIANA: Wow, this is great.

00:24:55.292 --> 00:24:56.761
MAN: And in this all documents,

00:24:56.761 --> 00:24:59.397
you have all the phone numbers
and some emails of those guys.

00:24:59.397 --> 00:25:00.765
So you can try.

00:25:00.765 --> 00:25:02.934
MARIANA: So they've all
actually been indicted, these?

00:25:02.934 --> 00:25:06.804
MAN: Yeah. We have the name
of some offshore companies

00:25:06.804 --> 00:25:10.274
used by those guys
of the Bandit 6.

00:25:10.274 --> 00:25:12.476
MARIANA: There's evidence of
money laundering in this one.

00:25:12.476 --> 00:25:13.978
MAN: Yeah, no doubt about it.

00:25:13.978 --> 00:25:15.679
MARIANA: And here
you have the whole list

00:25:15.679 --> 00:25:18.115
of the crewmen from this boat.

00:25:18.115 --> 00:25:22.287
You have the captain,
the chief engineer, the cook,

00:25:22.287 --> 00:25:23.954
and then all the officers.

00:25:23.954 --> 00:25:26.291
There's phone numbers
for this one, too.

00:25:26.291 --> 00:25:32.230
Passport numbers,
lots of photos, bank accounts.

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.132
There's just so much here.

00:25:34.132 --> 00:25:37.135
You see that the chief engineer
and the officer and the captain

00:25:37.135 --> 00:25:39.036
are all Spanish.

00:25:39.036 --> 00:25:42.173
And there are Spanish numbers
associated with them.

00:25:42.173 --> 00:25:43.675
This is big--

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:45.710
lists of names and numbers

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:48.879
of people directly involved
in the Bandit 6

00:25:48.879 --> 00:25:54.085
and who might know what illegal
activities are still ongoing.

00:25:54.085 --> 00:25:56.154
(phone ringing)

00:26:11.703 --> 00:26:13.804
(beep beep beep)

00:26:13.804 --> 00:26:15.273
MARIANA: Calling another person

00:26:15.273 --> 00:26:18.176
that we know worked
one of the Bandit 6 boats.

00:26:29.988 --> 00:26:31.322
(beep beep beep)

00:26:31.322 --> 00:26:32.223
MARIANA: He hung up.

00:26:39.097 --> 00:26:39.997
MARIANA: Okay.

00:26:39.997 --> 00:26:42.400
MAN: No, no, no, no, no. No, no.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:43.501
(ringing)

00:26:43.501 --> 00:26:44.869
MARIANA:
Pick up, pick up, pick up.

00:26:50.541 --> 00:26:51.642
MARIANA: Two and half hours now,

00:26:51.642 --> 00:26:53.411
we've been making
call after call.

00:26:53.411 --> 00:26:54.613
(ringing)

00:27:20.404 --> 00:27:21.773
¿Hola?

00:27:36.087 --> 00:27:40.625
MARIANA: So, today,
this afternoon, here in Vigo.

00:27:40.625 --> 00:27:41.726
Great.

00:27:44.195 --> 00:27:47.031
I finally secured
a face-to-face meeting

00:27:47.031 --> 00:27:50.901
with a former crew member
from a Bandit 6 fishing boat.

00:27:50.901 --> 00:27:54.973
He's texted me a location
on the outskirts of the city.

00:27:58.543 --> 00:28:00.277
I think this is it.

00:28:00.277 --> 00:28:02.046
Okay, here we are.

00:28:02.046 --> 00:28:04.615
♪ ♪

00:28:04.615 --> 00:28:06.183
I think it's here.

00:28:06.183 --> 00:28:12.390
♪ ♪

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:18.596
♪ ♪

00:28:18.596 --> 00:28:25.002
♪ ♪

00:28:25.002 --> 00:28:30.174
♪ ♪

00:28:30.174 --> 00:28:31.575
¿Hola?

00:28:31.575 --> 00:28:34.244
♪ ♪

00:28:34.244 --> 00:28:35.346
¿Hola?

00:28:35.346 --> 00:28:39.017
♪ ♪

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:46.124
♪ ♪

00:29:06.677 --> 00:29:08.045
MARIANA:
The man I'm talking to--

00:29:08.045 --> 00:29:09.814
we'll call him Luis--

00:29:09.814 --> 00:29:12.784
spent time working on one
of the Bandit 6 boats,

00:29:12.784 --> 00:29:15.453
as well as dozens
of other Spanish vessels

00:29:15.453 --> 00:29:16.921
throughout his career.

00:29:16.921 --> 00:29:20.625
He's my key to understanding
how illegal fishing works

00:29:20.625 --> 00:29:22.827
at an international level.

00:30:12.644 --> 00:30:13.878
MARIANA: Wow.

00:30:19.116 --> 00:30:22.086
MARIANA: We have no knowledge
that any of the ships we boarded

00:30:22.086 --> 00:30:25.423
had double bottoms
or illegal nets.

00:30:25.423 --> 00:30:28.392
But it sounds like that was
standard operating procedure

00:30:28.392 --> 00:30:31.495
on the Spanish ships
that Luis worked on.

00:30:31.495 --> 00:30:35.900
And that wasn't the only way
they skirted regulations.

00:31:38.529 --> 00:31:39.497
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:32:13.564 --> 00:32:14.632
MARIANA: Mm-hmm.

00:32:17.468 --> 00:32:20.337
Luis explains that within
the fishing industry,

00:32:20.337 --> 00:32:23.874
it's the shipowners who have
the power to make change.

00:32:23.874 --> 00:32:25.476
But those are the same folks

00:32:25.476 --> 00:32:28.713
who are profiting the most
off the status quo.

00:32:57.174 --> 00:32:58.642
MARIANA: He's right.

00:32:58.642 --> 00:33:00.177
The real issue is not the need

00:33:00.177 --> 00:33:03.481
for more regulations
or enforcement.

00:33:03.481 --> 00:33:08.352
It's our relationship
to the oceans themselves.

00:33:08.352 --> 00:33:12.523
I've been so focused on trying
to understand the black market--

00:33:12.523 --> 00:33:13.824
boat owners exploiting

00:33:13.824 --> 00:33:16.627
a disorganized
international system,

00:33:16.627 --> 00:33:18.929
fishermen using altered nets,

00:33:18.929 --> 00:33:22.032
captains turning off
AIS trackers--

00:33:22.032 --> 00:33:24.402
that I've overlooked
the obvious.

00:33:24.402 --> 00:33:29.673
A bigger, more devastating truth
about industrial fishing.

00:33:29.673 --> 00:33:34.712
SYLVIA EARLE: The impact of
illegal fishing is enormous,

00:33:34.712 --> 00:33:38.182
but it is relatively small

00:33:38.182 --> 00:33:43.487
compared to the impact
of legal fishing.

00:33:43.487 --> 00:33:45.922
MARIANA: Sylvia Earle
is an oceanographer

00:33:45.922 --> 00:33:48.126
and the founder of Mission Blue,

00:33:48.126 --> 00:33:51.729
a non-profit dedicated
to protecting our oceans.

00:33:51.729 --> 00:33:54.832
She doesn't mince words
when it comes to her opinions

00:33:54.832 --> 00:33:56.867
about the global
fishing industry.

00:33:56.867 --> 00:34:03.040
SYLVIA: This is the biggest
wildlife trade on the planet.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.710
We're waging war on the ocean.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:08.546
And we're doing it
with a big smile on our face,

00:34:08.546 --> 00:34:13.284
thinking, "This is great.
We have technologies now

00:34:13.284 --> 00:34:15.352
that can go to places
we couldn't go before,

00:34:15.352 --> 00:34:18.823
finding fish that were
not accessible before."

00:34:20.758 --> 00:34:23.861
MARIANA: Her words echo
so much of what I've seen

00:34:23.861 --> 00:34:25.963
over the last few months.

00:34:25.963 --> 00:34:28.098
PETER: Shrimp is
maybe not even 1/10

00:34:28.098 --> 00:34:29.400
of what the catch is, right?

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:32.135
And that's pretty typical
of a shrimp fishery,

00:34:32.135 --> 00:34:35.039
this enormous amount of bycatch.

00:34:35.039 --> 00:34:36.340
I, I always refer to bycatch

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:39.376
as the invisible victims
of industrial fishing.

00:34:39.376 --> 00:34:43.180
MARIANA: The invisible victims
of industrial fishing.

00:34:43.180 --> 00:34:45.816
In other words,
a waste of animal life

00:34:45.816 --> 00:34:49.286
like I've never seen before.

00:34:49.286 --> 00:34:53.390
It's difficult to watch
and even harder to fathom

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:57.994
that it's all legal,
happening every day and night

00:34:57.994 --> 00:35:00.564
on trawlers around the globe.

00:35:00.564 --> 00:35:02.099
 NEWS ANCHOR:
 The stocks of tropical tuna

00:35:02.099 --> 00:35:04.602
 in the Indian Ocean
 have been overfished.

00:35:04.602 --> 00:35:06.637
 NEWS ANCHOR: Human exploitation
 of the world's oceans

00:35:06.637 --> 00:35:08.272
 is passing the point
 of no return.

00:35:08.272 --> 00:35:09.540
 ANNOUNCER: Endless Shrimp.

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:10.908
 It's as much as you like,
 any way you like.

00:35:10.908 --> 00:35:12.643
 NEWS ANCHOR: A huge wealth
 of creatures and habitats

00:35:12.643 --> 00:35:15.679
 are under threat,
 decimated by overfishing.

00:35:15.679 --> 00:35:18.148
 ANNOUNCER: Our awesome,
 mouthwatering snow crab dinner,

00:35:18.148 --> 00:35:19.149
 for only $9.99.

00:35:19.149 --> 00:35:20.484
 ANNOUNCER:
 Have it all this summer

00:35:20.484 --> 00:35:22.253
 with the Crab Daddy Feast.

00:35:38.369 --> 00:35:40.871
PAUL: Industrial and commercial
fishing is scaled up so much

00:35:40.871 --> 00:35:45.009
that it really is a lot more
like mining than fishing.

00:35:45.009 --> 00:35:46.777
These nets are enormous.

00:35:46.777 --> 00:35:48.045
And they just take everything.

00:35:48.045 --> 00:35:53.551
All the fish within a kilometer
out of the water all at once.

00:35:53.551 --> 00:35:59.423
You're literally mining
the ocean of fish, of all life.

00:35:59.423 --> 00:36:02.159
SYLVIA: We have the capacity
to deplete wildlife

00:36:02.159 --> 00:36:07.832
on a scale
that is unprecedented.

00:36:07.832 --> 00:36:11.268
Technology is available
to almost everyone everywhere

00:36:11.268 --> 00:36:15.206
to find, capture, consume,
or market ocean wildlife

00:36:15.206 --> 00:36:17.040
on a scale that is simply,

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:19.910
no matter how you want
to measure it,

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:22.947
it's not sustainable.

00:36:22.947 --> 00:36:26.217
MARIANA: It's this future
that has me worried.

00:36:26.217 --> 00:36:29.120
But even today, there are
plenty of warning signs

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:30.855
if you know where to look.

00:36:30.855 --> 00:36:34.458
The experts I spoke with pointed
me to the coast of Ghana,

00:36:34.458 --> 00:36:37.228
where a local fishing community
has been transformed

00:36:37.228 --> 00:36:40.097
in ways I'm told
I won't believe.

00:36:40.097 --> 00:36:42.132
♪ ♪

00:36:42.132 --> 00:36:43.301
(beep)
(woman shouts)

00:36:51.508 --> 00:36:57.548
♪ ♪

00:36:57.548 --> 00:36:59.083
♪ ♪

00:36:59.083 --> 00:37:00.617
MARIANA: We got a call
late last night

00:37:00.617 --> 00:37:03.387
saying to be at the port
at 5 a.m. this morning

00:37:03.387 --> 00:37:07.825
because they had something
that they wanted to show us.

00:37:07.825 --> 00:37:09.059
Um...

00:37:09.059 --> 00:37:12.930
we're still not sure what
we'll be able to film or see,

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:16.867
but all we know is that
we're meeting this person

00:37:16.867 --> 00:37:19.637
in about five minutes.

00:37:19.637 --> 00:37:23.407
The call came from a local
fisherman turned crime boss

00:37:23.407 --> 00:37:26.510
who's known simply
as the Chairman.

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:28.980
I was introduced to him
by a contact of mine,

00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:32.550
but the Chairman has been
difficult to pin down.

00:37:35.252 --> 00:37:39.189
I see a couple of boats
with lights here.

00:37:39.189 --> 00:37:41.258
People working inside.
Let's check.

00:37:41.258 --> 00:37:42.693
(unbuckles seatbelt)

00:37:47.164 --> 00:37:48.266
(car door slams)

00:37:50.901 --> 00:37:57.107
♪ ♪

00:37:57.107 --> 00:38:01.145
♪ ♪

00:38:01.145 --> 00:38:02.679
Over here?

00:38:02.679 --> 00:38:03.781
♪ ♪

00:38:03.781 --> 00:38:04.782
(man talking)

00:38:04.782 --> 00:38:06.283
MARIANA: Is this it?

00:38:06.283 --> 00:38:08.419
(man talking)

00:38:10.420 --> 00:38:11.689
MARIANA: I guess this is it.

00:38:11.689 --> 00:38:13.024
Yeah, come in?

00:38:16.593 --> 00:38:20.297
Looks like that's
the Chairman on the phone.

00:38:20.297 --> 00:38:22.400
His name is Justice.

00:38:25.202 --> 00:38:27.538
MARIANA: Yeah, very fast.
We're very fast.

00:38:27.538 --> 00:38:29.239
Okay, we have to go, guys!

00:38:29.239 --> 00:38:32.943
♪ ♪

00:38:32.943 --> 00:38:34.512
We pass by one fishing boat

00:38:34.512 --> 00:38:39.150
that strangely doesn't have
any fishing gear.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.019
They are clearly unhappy
about our cameras.

00:38:42.019 --> 00:38:46.857
♪ ♪

00:38:46.857 --> 00:38:49.527
♪ ♪

00:38:49.527 --> 00:38:53.330
This is one of the boats
that came out from sea today?

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:54.798
Can we stop there
and talk to them?

00:38:54.798 --> 00:38:58.769
It's another fishing boat,
again with no fishing gear.

00:39:01.705 --> 00:39:08.345
♪ ♪

00:39:08.345 --> 00:39:10.948
MARIANA: Oh, wow.

00:39:10.948 --> 00:39:11.882
Look at that.

00:39:11.882 --> 00:39:16.920
It's all big blocks
of frozen fish.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:19.790
So the whole boat is fill,
is all full of that?

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:21.925
CHAIRMAN: Yes.

00:39:21.925 --> 00:39:24.695
MARIANA: These Ghanaians
used to fish for themselves,

00:39:24.695 --> 00:39:26.697
but an influx
of foreign trawlers

00:39:26.697 --> 00:39:29.133
has caused fish stocks
to plummet.

00:39:29.133 --> 00:39:32.702
So, now, in a perverse twist
of their livelihood,

00:39:32.702 --> 00:39:36.273
these fishermen go out to sea
and buy blocks of fish

00:39:36.273 --> 00:39:40.811
that have been caught and frozen
by the trawlers themselves.

00:39:41.678 --> 00:39:42.880
This came out from the sea?

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:43.847
CHAIRMAN: Yeah.

00:39:43.847 --> 00:39:45.483
MARIANA: From the trawlers?
CHAIRMAN: Yeah.

00:39:46.517 --> 00:39:47.784
MARIANA: Yeah.

00:39:47.784 --> 00:39:49.353
So they would throw it away,
and instead...

00:39:49.353 --> 00:39:50.554
CHAIRMAN: Yeah.

00:39:50.554 --> 00:39:51.989
MARIANA: ...the fishermen
go out and pick it.

00:39:51.989 --> 00:39:53.190
CHAIRMAN: Yeah.

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:55.392
MARIANA: How far
are the Chinese vessels?

00:39:55.392 --> 00:39:56.426
CHAIRMAN: 12 hours.

00:39:56.426 --> 00:39:58.763
MARIANA: 12 hours to get there?

00:40:00.364 --> 00:40:02.666
Through forces
beyond his control,

00:40:02.666 --> 00:40:06.170
the Chairman has been dragged
into an illegal seafood trade

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:08.572
known as Saiko.

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:12.476
You have been working
as a fisherman

00:40:12.476 --> 00:40:14.077
for a very long time.

00:40:14.077 --> 00:40:15.913
MARIANA: You're originally
an artisanal fisher.

00:40:15.913 --> 00:40:16.814
CHAIRMAN: Yeah, yeah.

00:40:16.814 --> 00:40:19.382
MARIANA: And so,
since this is illegal,

00:40:19.382 --> 00:40:21.451
why did you agree to talk to us?

00:40:21.451 --> 00:40:22.553
CHAIRMAN: Well...

00:40:26.891 --> 00:40:27.992
MARIANA: It's not.

00:40:29.559 --> 00:40:30.895
MARIANA: Right, that it
helps a lot of people.

00:40:30.895 --> 00:40:31.728
CHAIRMAN: Yeah.

00:40:31.728 --> 00:40:32.996
MARIANA: So, what do you
say to people

00:40:32.996 --> 00:40:35.432
who say what the trawlers
are doing

00:40:35.432 --> 00:40:37.067
is that they are
destroying the oceans,

00:40:37.067 --> 00:40:38.736
they're killing all this fish,

00:40:38.736 --> 00:40:40.003
and you are
contributing to that

00:40:40.003 --> 00:40:42.540
because you are buying
this bycatch?

00:40:46.043 --> 00:40:47.011
MARIANA: Right.

00:40:53.217 --> 00:40:54.118
MARIANA: Right.

00:40:59.356 --> 00:41:02.192
MARIANA: The reality
is even more depressing.

00:41:02.192 --> 00:41:05.863
What started out as foreign
trawlers selling their bycatch

00:41:05.863 --> 00:41:09.466
has become so established
and so profitable

00:41:09.466 --> 00:41:10.967
that many foreign ships

00:41:10.967 --> 00:41:14.805
are now purposely targeting
local fish, which, of course,

00:41:14.805 --> 00:41:19.076
has only made
the overfishing problem worse.

00:41:19.076 --> 00:41:23.580
This is the trickle-down effect
of our global fishing industry--

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:27.718
a local population so powerless
to stop the overfishing

00:41:27.718 --> 00:41:31.555
that it's now been forced
to embrace a black market.

00:41:31.555 --> 00:41:34.424
(crowd murmur)

00:41:34.424 --> 00:41:35.559
Oh, wow.
So, this is all the...

00:41:35.559 --> 00:41:36.593
CHAIRMAN: Yes. Yeah.

00:41:36.593 --> 00:41:37.761
This is the Saiko fish.

00:41:37.761 --> 00:41:39.062
MARIANA: Another one there.

00:41:39.062 --> 00:41:42.265
Make no mistake about what
you're looking at here.

00:41:42.265 --> 00:41:47.404
This is a fishing town that now
survives on frozen fish.

00:41:47.404 --> 00:41:48.872
I mean, it's incredible.

00:41:48.872 --> 00:41:51.842
You look around, and all of it
is basically Saiko.

00:41:51.842 --> 00:41:56.480
♪ ♪

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:59.950
♪ ♪

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:01.785
You can see, it's just
boat after boat,

00:42:01.785 --> 00:42:03.320
and all of them
are filled with fish,

00:42:03.320 --> 00:42:07.457
and they're bringing
all the fish now to shore.

00:42:07.457 --> 00:42:11.528
They cover it with blankets to
keep it cold, to keep it frozen.

00:42:11.528 --> 00:42:13.330
Oh, wow! Look at that!

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:14.899
Holy (bleep).

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:18.368
That is insane!

00:42:18.368 --> 00:42:21.004
So much.

00:42:21.004 --> 00:42:22.773
So you can see
how big this boat is.

00:42:22.773 --> 00:42:24.174
CHAIRMAN: Yeah, yeah.

00:42:24.174 --> 00:42:27.144
MARIANA: And then every single
one of these, basically, holds,

00:42:27.144 --> 00:42:30.481
is filled, filled, filled
with frozen fish.

00:42:30.481 --> 00:42:31.582
CHAIRMAN: Yes.

00:42:53.637 --> 00:42:56.206
MARIANA: Yeah, it's out.
It's open.

00:42:56.206 --> 00:43:01.578
♪ ♪

00:43:01.578 --> 00:43:03.346
PAUL: We can't do this forever.

00:43:03.346 --> 00:43:07.550
There will come a point
where we don't have fish.

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:09.052
And the people that will suffer

00:43:09.052 --> 00:43:11.221
are the people that go out
to fish every day

00:43:11.221 --> 00:43:13.256
to feed their families.

00:43:13.256 --> 00:43:15.559
But it won't be the owners
of the companies

00:43:15.559 --> 00:43:16.626
that took all the fish.

00:43:16.626 --> 00:43:18.829
They'll just go
do something else.

00:43:29.473 --> 00:43:32.742
MARIANA: I'm almost embarrassed
to admit how blind I've been

00:43:32.742 --> 00:43:36.713
to the ugly truth about
our global fishing industry.

00:43:36.713 --> 00:43:40.683
But I can't unsee
what I've seen on this journey.

00:43:40.683 --> 00:43:44.087
The industry needs to change.

00:43:44.087 --> 00:43:45.755
We all do.

00:43:45.755 --> 00:43:49.159
SYLVIA: Taking wild animals
from the ocean

00:43:49.159 --> 00:43:53.330
and turning it into money
or products...

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:56.299
there's a cost.

00:43:56.299 --> 00:43:59.736
There's a cost to everyone.

00:43:59.736 --> 00:44:01.438
It's not free.

00:44:01.438 --> 00:44:06.477
♪ ♪

